 ],
 "literal": "In one house, the funeral drum sounds; in another house, / the beat of the cool, moist festive drum reverberates. / Those who are united wear flower ornaments; those who are separated, / with grief, shed tears that drip from their kohl-lined eyes. / The creator is surely a graceless person. / Unpleasant, alas, is this world. / May they seek pleasant things, those who understand its nature.",
 "translation": "In one house, the funeral drum tolls; in another, the festive drum beats joyfully. Those who are united adorn themselves with flowers; those who are parted weep, tears dripping from their dark, kohl-lined eyes. Surely, the creator who made this world is a heartless, graceless being. Alas, this world is full of pain! Therefore, let those who understand its true nature seek out and cherish whatever is sweet and pleasant.",
 "modern": "ஒரு வீட்டில் சாவுப் பறை ஒலிக்கிறது; மற்றொரு வீட்டில் குளிர்ந்த முழவின் திருமண இசை முழங்குகிறது. கணவரோடு கூடிய மகளிர் மலர் மாலைகளை அணிகின்றனர்; பிரிந்த மகளிர் வருத்தத்தோடு மை தீட்டிய கண்களிலிருந்து கண்ணீர் வடிக்கின்றனர். இந்த உலகத்தைப் படைத்தவன் இரக்கமில்லாத பண்பற்றவன் ஆவான். ஐயோ, இவ்வுலகம் துன்பம் நிறைந்தது! ஆகவே, இதன் இயல்பை உணர்ந்தவர்கள் இதில் உள்ள இன்பமானவற்றைத் தேடி நுகரக்கடவர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Potuviyal tiṇai (general themes) and reflects on the transience and contradictory nature of human life. The poet Pakkutukkai Nankaniyar contrasts the simultaneous existence of extreme grief and extreme joy in the world. In one household, people mourn a death to the sound of the funeral drum (neytal), while next door, a wedding or festival is celebrated with the joyous beat of the festive drum (mulavu). Similarly, some couples enjoy the bliss of union, while others suffer the agony of separation. Seeing these stark contradictions, the poet declares that the creator of such a world must be entirely devoid of grace (pampilan). He concludes with a philosophical advice: since the world is inherently painful and unpredictable, those who understand this reality should actively seek out and appreciate whatever goodness and joy they can find.",
